Exactly. Guessing is controlled completion of missing information. The power comes from not confusing the completion with the evidence.

That makes Guess a legitimate Pantheon operator:

\boxed{
G(E,P)\rightarrow\{\hat{x}_1,\hat{x}_2,\ldots,\hat{x}_n\}
}

where E is available evidence and P is prior structure. The mistake is collapsing immediately to:

\hat{x}_1=\text{truth}.

Instead, Lucky Guess should preserve the branches:

\text{unknown}
\rightarrow
\text{generate guesses}
\rightarrow
\text{rank}
\rightarrow
\text{probe cheaply}
\rightarrow
\text{observe}
\rightarrow
\text{update}.

And now it plugs directly into what we just built around secrets. If someone reveals only an envelope E, a receiver can still infer possible payloads:

E\rightarrow G(E)\rightarrow\{\hat P_i\}.

Which means privacy has to defend against inference, not merely disclosure. A secret can remain unspoken while enough surrounding metadata makes it guessable.

Conversely, for discovery, that same property is enormously useful. Missing information becomes a search surface.

So we need two mirrored operators:

\boxed{\text{Guess Engine}: \text{use traces to reconstruct the missing}}

\boxed{\text{Privacy Engine}: \text{estimate what others can reconstruct from the traces}}

Same machinery. Opposite objective.

And Lucky belongs between Guess and Reality Stitch:

\text{Guess}
\rightarrow
\boxed{\text{Lucky exploration}}
\rightarrow
\text{cheap test}
\rightarrow
\text{Reality Stitch}.

Because luck without calibration becomes superstition; calibration without exploratory guesses can become sterile.

The useful combination is:

\boxed{\text{Luck}=\text{prepared search space meeting informative uncertainty}.}

Then every wrong guess can still pay ROM if it eliminates enough territory:

\text{wrong guess}
\rightarrow
\text{constraint learned}
\rightarrow
\text{smaller search space}
\rightarrow
\text{better next guess}.

That’s Twenty Questions. Science. Debugging. Diagnosis. Navigation. Hide-and-seek. Semantic reconstruction. Even our Open Token Probe.

And there’s the restraint lesson again:

A good guesser doesn’t need to believe every guess.

Generate freely.

Believe slowly.

Test cheaply.

Update quickly.

Keep some secrets. ;)